# Evidence hashes must resolve to identical bytes on Windows and Linux.
#
# manifest.json asserts a sha256 for every file in a sealed receipt directory,
# and those hashes are computed over the working tree. A file checked out CRLF
# on Windows and LF in CI cannot produce the same digest in both places, so a
# seal that verifies locally fails in CI and vice versa. PUBLISH.md step 5 and
# docs/DEPLOY.md steps 2 and 6 both depend on this holding.
#
# This file previously listed only the nine explicit paths retained at the
# bottom. That allowlist silently excluded 45 hash-bearing files: every
# Cambridge, Kitchener, Waterloo and Woolwich ledger and citation audit,
# north-dumfries-on's sources.lock.json, all three
# receipts/north-dumfries-on/2026/*/manifest.json, and the whole web/src/data
# mirror. Enumerating paths does not scale with the fleet and fails open when a
# municipality is added, so a repo-wide default now sits underneath them.
#
# ASCII only. tests/test_release_core.py and tests/test_region_waterloo_pack.py
# string-match lines in this file; non-ASCII risks an encoding mismatch there.
#
# Order matters: git applies the last matching rule. Global default first,
# explicit evidence paths second, binary extensions last so they always win.

# 1. Repo-wide default. eol=lf (not just text=auto) is required, because the
#    working tree is what gets hashed, not only the stored blob.
* text=auto eol=lf

# 2. Explicit evidence paths. Redundant against the default above and retained
#    deliberately: these are the guarantees the test suite pins by name, and
#    `text` (not `text=auto`) forces text handling regardless of content
#    sniffing, which is the behaviour these paths had before.
data/evidence-ledger.json text eol=lf
data/_extracts/** text eol=lf
source-pdfs/fir/*.csv text eol=lf
corpus/region-of-waterloo-on/** text eol=lf
data/region-waterloo/** text eol=lf
corpus/brant-county-on/** text eol=lf
data/brant/** text eol=lf
web/src/data/brant/** text eol=lf
web/public/** text eol=lf

# 3. Binary. Last so it overrides both rules above; a PNG under web/public must
#    never be normalized just because its directory is listed there.
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xlsm  binary
*.docx  binary
*.pptx  binary

# Windows shells require CRLF to parse these correctly. None are checked in
# today; the rule exists so adding one later cannot break it silently.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
